1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,040
The ECG measures voltage differences generated by the combined
activity of many cells. It helps describe timing and the direction of
activation. It is not a recording from one cell, and it is not a
pressure transducer or a direct measure of contractile strength.

2
00:00:17,040 --> 00:00:35,016
Keep the distinction throughout this chapter: cellular events help
explain the tracing, while pulse and perfusion explain the patient. A
technically organized tracing may coexist with very poor output.
Conversely, a noisy tracing may occur in someone with a normal pulse.

3
00:00:35,016 --> 00:00:54,504
Activation normally begins in the sinoatrial node and spreads through
the atria. The atrioventricular node introduces a delay before the
signal travels through the His bundle, right and left bundle branches,
and Purkinje fibres. This sequence supports ventricular filling before
ventricular contraction.

4
00:00:54,504 --> 00:01:11,880
A delay is not automatically a block, and a backup pacemaker is not
automatically a new disease. Ask whether the normal pathway is
working, whether the impulse is conducted reliably, and whether a
lower structure has taken over because the dominant pacemaker is
suppressed.

5
00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:29,712
The school uses a hierarchy of about sixty to one hundred per minute
for sinus pacing, forty to sixty for the AV junction, and twenty to
forty for a ventricular escape focus. These are teaching ranges for
rhythm recognition, not guaranteed rates or treatment thresholds.

6
00:01:29,712 --> 00:01:47,328
An escape rhythm arrives because the expected dominant impulse did not
take control. A premature ectopic beat arrives early. Timing therefore
matters as much as the shape of the complex when you decide whether a
lower pacemaker is protecting the circulation or competing with it.

7
00:01:47,328 --> 00:02:07,104
For a working ventricular muscle cell, phase zero is a rapid sodium-
dependent depolarization. Phase one is brief early repolarization.
During phase two, calcium entry and potassium exit support a plateau.
Phase three repolarization is followed by the resting phase four.

8
00:02:07,104 --> 00:02:26,256
Do not apply that ventricular diagram unchanged to nodal pacemaker
cells. Their automaticity and upstroke differ. The school links
sodium-channel blockers, calcium-channel effects and class-three
antiarrhythmics to these processes conceptually; that does not confer
authority to administer those drugs.

9
00:02:26,256 --> 00:02:46,272
The QRS reflects the summed sequence of ventricular depolarization.
The ST segment broadly corresponds to ventricular cells remaining
depolarized during the plateau, and the T wave reflects recovery. A
nearly flat ST segment does not mean that the myocardium has no
electrical activity.

10
00:02:46,272 --> 00:03:05,664
The school simplifies cellular phases into surface correlates. Do not
turn this into a rule that every phase produces its own visible mark,
particularly a post-QRS notch for phase one. Lead direction, timing
across many cells and conduction differences shape the surface signal.
